---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/history/foundational/cradles-of-civilisation');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const timelineCfg = {
  unit: 'year',
  events: [
    { year: -3200, title: 'Uruk — the first city', text: 'Irrigation canals in southern Mesopotamia feed a settlement of tens of thousands. Writing is invented here, to track grain and taxes.', tag: 'Mesopotamia' },
    { year: -3100, title: 'Egypt unifies', text: 'Upper and Lower Egypt join under the first pharaohs. The pyramid age follows within a few centuries.', tag: 'Egypt' },
    { year: -2600, title: 'Indus cities on a grid', text: 'Harappa and Mohenjo-daro are laid out with precise street grids and covered drains — urban planning ahead of its time.', tag: 'Indus' },
    { year: -1750, title: 'Hammurabi\u2019s code', text: 'Babylon\u2019s king inscribes 282 laws on a stone stele. Justice becomes a written, public thing.', tag: 'Mesopotamia' },
    { year: -1200, title: 'Bronze Age collapse', text: 'Drought, raids and cascading failures end palace economies across the Eastern Mediterranean within a single lifetime.', tag: 'Mediterranean' },
    { year: -550, title: 'The Achaemenid method', text: 'Cyrus\u2019 Persia runs the largest empire yet — on roads, provincial governors, and tolerated local customs.', tag: 'Mediterranean' },
    { year: -331, title: 'Alexander reaches Persepolis', text: 'The Greek and Persian worlds fuse, opening three centuries of Hellenistic exchange from Egypt to India.', tag: 'Mediterranean' },
    { year: -27, title: 'Rome becomes an empire', text: 'Augustus ends the republic. Roads, law and sea lanes knit three continents into one market.', tag: 'Mediterranean' },
    { year: 476, title: 'The west falls, the east persists', text: 'Odoacer deposes the last western emperor. The eastern half carries the Roman name for another thousand years.', tag: 'Mediterranean' },
  ],
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            Civilisation is a chain reaction. Farming produces <strong>surplus food</strong>;
            surplus lets some people stop farming; specialists — scribes, smiths, priests,
            soldiers — build <strong>cities</strong>; cities need <strong>records, laws and
            rulers</strong>. Every cradle of civilisation, from the Nile to the Indus, runs the
            same sequence with local accents.
          </p>
          <p>
            The puzzle is why the reaction took so long to start. Anatomically modern humans
            have existed for roughly 300,000 years, but farming begins only about 12,000 years
            ago and the first cities only about 6,000 — meaning humans spent 95% of their
            species' history <em>not</em> building civilisations. The best current answer is
            that the end of the last Ice Age (~11,700 years ago) created the stable, warm,
            CO₂-rich climate in which wild cereals grew densely enough in a few lucky places —
            the Fertile Crescent, the Yangtze valley, highland Mexico — that settling down and
            tending them beat moving around. Farming was invented independently at least
            half a dozen times, which is history's strongest hint that whenever the conditions
            line up, the chain reaction follows.
          </p>
          <p>
            Watch it happen below. Drag the scrubber — everything to its right hasn't happened
            yet:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>three and a half millennia, one scrubber — filter by region with the chips</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="timeline" data-config={JSON.stringify(timelineCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>The Neolithic revolution: farming changes everything</h2>
          <p>
            The archaeologist V. Gordon Childe called it a <em>revolution</em>, and the label
            sticks for its consequences even though the transition took centuries. Domesticating
            wheat and barley (Fertile Crescent, ~9500 BCE), rice (Yangtze, ~7000 BCE) and maize
            (Mesoamerica, ~7000 BCE) meant people could stay put, store food, and — crucially —
            support more children, since settled mothers wean earlier than carrying-foragers.
            Villages swelled into towns; stored grain became wealth that could be owned, stolen
            and defended; and the first hard social hierarchies grew around whoever controlled
            the granary. The costs were real, too: dense settled populations became disease
            incubators (most epidemic infections jumped to us from domesticated animals), and
            skeletons show early farmers were typically shorter and worse-fed than their hunter
            ancestors. Farming was less a leap upward than a trap that paid in numbers —
            which, in history, is usually how revolutions actually work.
          </p>

          <h2>Six cradles, one pattern</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Mesopotamia</strong>, "the land between the rivers" (Tigris and Euphrates),
            went first because irrigation there turned silt into wheat on an industrial scale.
            By about 3400–3100 BCE, Uruk held tens of thousands of people — the first true city
            — ruled by priests and, later, kings, worshipping in stepped ziggurats, organised
            into rival city-states that traded as ferociously as they fought. <strong>Egypt</strong>
            followed a river with better manners: the Nile's annual flood arrived almost
            punctually, so a single state could organise it. Upper and Lower Egypt unified
            under the first pharaohs around 3100 BCE, and by ~2550 BCE the Giza pyramids rose —
            the tallest structures on Earth for the next four millennia. <strong>The Indus
            valley</strong> built Harappa and Mohenjo-daro around 2600 BCE on precise street
            grids with covered drains, standardised brick sizes and weights — the ancient world's
            best plumbing and, oddly, no evidence of kings or palaces; its script remains
            undeciphered, and its cities emptied out around 1900 BCE, probably as rivers shifted
            and the monsoon weakened. <strong>China</strong>'s first dynasties grew along the
            Yellow River (the Shang, from ~1600 BCE, left "oracle bones" — the earliest Chinese
            writing, and it is already fully formed). In the <strong>Andes</strong>, Caral and
            its neighbours were building monumental platforms by 2600 BCE without pottery or
            wheat; and <strong>Mesoamerica</strong> raised the Olmec centres a millennium later,
            on maize. Six regions, three continents, no contact between them — one pattern.
            Historians have retired the phrase "the cradle" for exactly this reason: civilisation
            is something humans do when rivers and grain cooperate, not a torch handed down
            from a single origin.
          </p>
          <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "civilisation" is not a grade for being better than
          our ancestors. The foragers who preceded it often ate better, worked fewer hours and
          suffered fewer epidemics; what civilisation uniquely produced was scale — cities,
          writing, armies — and with it hierarchy, slavery, plague and organised war. Similarly,
          the pyramids were not built by gangs of whip-driven slaves: excavated workers'
          villages near Giza show paid, ration-fed, rotation-worked labourers, some buried with
          honours. The evidence matters more than the movie version.</p>

          <h2>Writing, law and the tools of the state</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Writing starts boring.</strong> Cuneiform — wedges pressed in clay — began
            in Uruk around 3200 BCE as accounting: receipts for grain, beer and livestock. Only
            centuries of reuse turned the system literary, eventually producing the
            <em>Epic of Gilgamesh</em>, the world's oldest great work of literature. Egypt's
            hieroglyphs and China's oracle-bone script grew from the same accounting-and-ritual
            root, which suggests a rule: <em>where surplus exists, someone counts it, and the
            counting becomes writing</em>. Scribes became history's first profession of
            specialists trained for decades — in Sumer, literally in schools called "tablet
            houses".
          </p>
          <p>
            Law followed the same logic: scale needs written rules. <strong>Hammurabi's
            code</strong>, inscribed on a stone stele in Babylon around 1754 BCE, published 282
            laws in public view — prices, wages, divorce, liability — with penalties graded by
            class (harm to a noble cost far more than the same harm to a commoner, and the
            famous "eye for an eye" applied mainly between equals). An earlier code, Ur-Nammu's
            (~2100 BCE), shows Hammurabi was refining a tradition, not inventing it. The deeper
            point stands: justice became a written, <em>public</em> thing — and a ruler's
            legitimacy now came partly from being seen to uphold predictable rules. It is the
            ancestor of every legal code you live under, and the stele itself (in the Louvre)
            is still a working argument about what a state owes its people.
          </p>

          <h2>Collapse and empire</h2>
          <p>
            The Bronze Age world — palace economies wired together by trade in copper and tin —
            took about two millennia to build and roughly one generation to unbuild. Around
            1200–1150 BCE, drought (visible in pollen cores and cave records), migrations and
            raids cascaded: the Hittite empire vanished, Ugarit burned with its tablets still in
            the oven, Mycenaean Greece emptied, and Egypt alone survived — recording its victories
            against the mysterious "Sea Peoples" at Medinet Habu in 1177 BCE. The classic
            lesson: <em>complex systems fail fast</em>. Interdependence is efficient until any
            link breaks, and then the whole web comes down together.
          </p>
          <p>
            Recovery produced bigger machines. Assyria ruled by terror and deportations; then
            Persia (from ~550 BCE) invented a template that lasted: Cyrus the Great governed the
            largest empire yet seen not by crushing it but by running it — the Royal Road with
            relay couriers, provincial governors (satraps) audited from the centre, local laws
            and cults tolerated. Alexander the Great conquered Persia in a decade (reaching
            Persepolis in 331 BCE), and though his empire split at his death, the Greek-Persian
            fusion it created — Hellenistic science, coinage and language from Egypt to India —
            lasted three centuries. Rome industrialised the model: roads totalling around
            80,000 km, a Mediterranean cleared of pirates, law applied uniformly, and (from 212
            CE) citizenship extended to nearly all free inhabitants. When the western half
            "fell" in 476 CE — Odoacer simply deposing one more emperor in an already-shrunk
            job — the eastern half carried the Roman name, law and language of government for
            another thousand years. Nothing fully ends; the "fall of Rome" is an argument, not
            a date.
          </p>

          <h2>The machinery of surplus: how you feed a city</h2>
  <p>
    The chain reaction deserves its engineering spelled out, because the numbers are the
    history. A forager needs roughly 10 km² of territory; a farmer can feed perhaps ten
    times more people from the same ground, and an irrigated farmer — controlling the water,
    replanting selectively — several times that again. Southern Mesopotamia's canal systems
    turned that arithmetic into Uruk: canals dug and dredged by corvée labour, grain
    collected as tax, stored in temple granaries, and paid out as daily rations of bread and
    beer (the standard wage, recorded in the same cuneiform that invented writing to track
    it). The specialists the surplus fed included bronze smiths — bronze, an alloy of copper
    and tin, needs ores that rarely occur together, so the Bronze Age ran on long-distance
    trade networks that scattered the era's cities like beads on a string, and whose
    fragility would one day be its undoing. Add the wheeled cart (~3500 BCE), the sail
    (~3500 BCE) and the potter's wheel, and the city becomes a machine for converting grain
    into everything grain-money can buy: walls, temples, armies and, within a few
    generations, professional scribes keeping the whole ledger balanced.
  </p>
  <p>
    Egypt ran the same machine on a more reliable river, and the contrast teaches as much as
    either alone. The Nile's flood was gentle, predictable and arrived at planting season —
    so one state, not rival city-states, could plan around it; the pharaoh's title
    <em>netjer-nefer</em>, "the good god", was partly an irrigation job description.
    Surveying fields after each flood (boundaries washed away annually) made Egypt the
    birthplace of practical geometry; the pyramids were logistics projects — feeding 10,000
    or more workers with bread, beer and onions on an industrial schedule — as much as
    monuments. The lesson generalises across every cradle: <em>infrastructure writes the
    politics</em>. Where water needed cooperation at scale, strong central states formed;
    where irrigation was local, competitive city-states did.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            How do we know any of this? Three toolkits. <strong>Archaeology</strong> reads
            mudbrick, potsherds and rubbish middens — the majority of ancient lives left no
            text, and a dig is often the only witness. <strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong>
            measures the decay of carbon-14 in organic material (usable back to about 50,000
            years, calibrated against tree rings to correct its wobble). <strong>Texts</strong>
            do the rest: several hundred thousand cuneiform tablets survive — most still
            untranslated, which makes Assyriology one of the few fields where the backlog is
            bigger than the workforce — and masterpieces keep emerging from them (Gilgamesh's
            flood tablet was only recognised in 1872, causing a sensation in Victorian London).
          </p>
          <p>
            Two debates give the flavour of the discipline. Climate: the "4.2 kiloyear event" —
            a centuries-long megadrought around 2200 BCE — coincides with the collapse of
            Egypt's Old Kingdom and the Akkadian empire, and how much weight to give climate
            versus human politics in such collapses is a live argument (one you will recognise
            from every modern headline). And periodisation: the philosopher Karl Jaspers argued
            that between 800 and 200 BCE — the "Axial Age" — Confucius, the Buddha, the Hebrew
            prophets and Greek philosophy all reframed morality independently, which is either
            the deepest pattern in the data or a seductive coincidence, depending on which
            historian you ask. That is precisely what history is: not a list, but an argument
            about causes run on evidence — and the timeline above, for all its tidy chips and
            scrubber, is really a table of exhibits.
          </p>

          <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kramer, <em>The Sumerians</em>; Van de Mieroop, <em>A History of the Ancient Near East</em>; Eric Cline, <em>1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed</em>; Diamond, <em>Guns, Germs, and Steel</em>; British Museum and Louvre object records (Hammurabi stele; oracle bones).</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
